  "worked_solution": "We begin by noticing that at $x=b$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f33639f6a54a2e0f35c52b684356ed832047ee538b8651e9ca9f75a542d366a9.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($\u2323$) at $x=c$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
